.\" The manual page for pdvim, the program. pdvim(5) is its configuration.
.\" View it with: man docs/man/pdvim.1
.TH PDVIM 1 2026-11-22 "pdvim" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pdvim \- a keyboard-driven PDF reader with vi key bindings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pdvim
.RI [ file ]...
.br
.B pdvim
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pdvim
reads PDF documents, EPUB and FB2 books, CBZ and CBT comics, XPS
documents, SVG drawings, and PNG, JPEG, GIF, TIFF, BMP and other images,
from the keyboard, with keys like those of
.BR vi (1),
and from the mouse.
It renders pages with MuPDF and lays them out in one continuous column,
in up to four side by side, or a page or two facing pages at a time.
.PP
Each
.I file
opens in a tab of its own.
Started without one, pdvim lists the documents read most recently, to pick
one from, or, with the
.B session
setting of pdvim(5), opens the tabs left open when it was last quit.
pdvim remembers the page, zoom, fit, rotation and columns of every
document it has opened, and starts each where it was left.
.PP
Books without pages of their own, EPUB and FB2, are laid out on pages the
size of the window, in the type size the
.B fontsize
setting gives, and laid out again when the window changes.
.PP
Highlights, underlines, strike-outs, squiggly underlines and notes made in
pdvim are standard PDF annotations, written into the file when it is saved
with
.BR :w ,
so other PDF readers show them too.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print pdvim's version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print how to start pdvim and exit, as for any other argument that starts
with
.BR \- .
.SH KEYS
Put a count before a key to repeat it, as in vi:
.B 3j
scrolls three steps down, and
.B 12G
goes to page 12.
These are the default keys of normal mode, reading.
pdvim(5) tells how to change them, and
.B :help
lists every key of every mode as bound at the time.
.SS Movement
.TP 18
.BR j ", " k ", " h ", " l
Scroll down, up, left, right; the arrow keys do too.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-d ", " Ctrl\-u
Scroll half a screen down, up.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-f ", " Space ", " Ctrl\-b ", " Shift\-Space
Scroll a full screen down, up.
.TP
.BR J ", " K
Go to the next, previous page; also
.BR PageDown ", " PageUp .
.TP
.BR gg ", " G
Go to the first, last page; with a count, to page
.IR n .
.TP
.IB n %
Go
.I n
percent of the way through the document.
.TP
.BR H ", " L
Go to the top, bottom of the page.
.TP
.BR ]] ", " [[
Go to the next, previous chapter or section.
.TP
.BR } ", " {
Bring the next paragraph, or the one above, to the top of the window.
A paragraph that runs on over a page is one paragraph.
.TP
.B Tab
Show the table of contents, to go to a chapter.
A document without one has one guessed from its text: from a printed
contents page, its page numbers found among the pages, or else from the
headings, told by their larger or bold type.
A guess can miss a heading or take in a line that is not one, and a
scanned document, with no text, has none.
.TP
.B go
Show every page small, side by side;
.BR h ", " j ", " k ", " l
move through them,
.B gg
and
.B G
go to the first and last, or with a count to page
.IR n ,
and
.B Enter
reads from the one selected.
.SS View
.TP 18
.BR + ", " \- ", " zi ", " zo
Zoom in, out.
In a book, make the type bigger or smaller instead.
.TP
.BR = ", " z0
Zoom to 100%;
.IB n =
zooms to
.IR n %.
.TP
.BR zw ", " zp
Fit the page width, or the whole page, to the window.
.TP
.B r
Rotate.
.TP
.B R
Turn recoloring for a dark background on or off.
.TP
.B t
Turn margin trimming on or off.
.TP
.B zb
Hide the tab and status bars, or show them.
.TP
.B zc
Show two pages side by side, or one, in this tab.
.TP
.B zm
Show pages continuously, one at a time, or two facing pages at a time,
in turn.
.TP
.B zf
Draw a line round every page, or stop.
.TP
.B F11
Fill the screen, or stop.
.SS Search and commands
.TP 18
.BR / ", " ?
Search forwards, backwards.
Case is ignored unless what is typed has a capital letter in it; the
.B smartcase
setting of pdvim(5) turns that off.
.TP
.BR n ", " N
Go to the next, previous match.
.TP
.B g/
List every match, each with its page and line; type to narrow it, enter
to go to one.
.TP
.B :
Open the command prompt.
.TP
.B .
Run the last command line again.
.TP
.B o
List the documents read recently.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-g
Show the document's title, author, dates and more.
.TP
.BR Esc ", " Ctrl\-c
Cancel a half-typed command, and hide search highlights.
.TP
.B q
Close the tab, and quit after the last one.
.SS Links and forms
.TP 18
.B f
Label every link, form field and marking in view; type a label to follow
the link, fill in the field, or pick the marking.
.TP
.B F
Label the links; type a label to open its place in a new tab.
.TP
.B ;y
Label the links; type a label to copy where it goes.
.TP
.BR gi ", " gI
Fill in the first, last form field in view;
.B Tab
goes on to the next.
.TP
.B g;
Go back to the form field last filled in.
.PP
While labels are shown,
.B /
and some text narrows them to what holds the text.
.SS Selecting text
.TP 18
.BR v ", " V
Label the lines in view; type a label to start selecting characters, or
whole lines, from there.
.TP
.B s
Type some text in view, then the label beside a match, to put a cursor
there; it moves without selecting until
.B v
or
.B V
starts a selection from it.
In visual mode,
.B s
moves the cursor to the match instead.
.TP
.B I
Label the pictures and figures in view; type a label to select one.
While the lines are labelled, after
.B v
or
.BR V ,
.B I
labels the figures instead.
.TP
.B gv
Select the last selection again.
.PP
In visual mode,
.BR h ", " j ", " k ", " l ,
.BR w ", " b ", " e ,
.BR 0 ", " ^ ", " $ ,
.BR f ", " F ", " t ", " T
and
.BR ; ", " ,
move the cursor as in vi, and
.B o
goes to the other end of the selection.
.B ip
selects the paragraph the cursor is in, and the next ones when pressed
again or after a count;
.B ap
does the same, and copies a blank line after.
.B iw
and
.B aw
select the word, and the space after it, and the next words when pressed
again or after a count;
.B is
and
.B as
do the same with sentences.
.BR * " and " #
search forward and backward for the selection, as a whole word if it is
one, as vim's
.B *
does for the word under the cursor.
.B y
copies the selection, as running text: the lines of a paragraph joined
with spaces, a word broken over two lines with a hyphen made whole, and
paragraphs kept apart by a blank line;
.BR H ,
.BR U ,
.B S
and
.B W
highlight, underline, strike out or squiggly-underline it;
.B n
puts a note beside it,
.B c
changes the note on a marking, and
.B d
takes markings away.
.B Esc
leaves.
.PP
.B I
labels the pictures and figures in view here too; typing a label selects
one as a box.
.B y
then copies it as a picture, as
.B yi
does,
.BR H ,
.BR U ,
.B S
and
.B W
mark its area, and a motion goes back to selecting text from the cursor.
.SS Highlights and other markings
.TP 18
.BR ]h ", " [h
Go to the next, previous marking.
.TP
.B gh
Label the markings in view; type a label to pick one.
.TP
.B d
Take away the marking picked.
.TP
.B c
Change the note on the marking picked.
.TP
.B Shift\-Tab
List the markings in a sidebar.
.TP
.BR u ", " Ctrl\-r
Take back the last change to the markings, or put it back.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-s
Save the markings into the file, as
.BR :w .
.SS Marks and jumps
.TP 18
.BI m letter
Mark this place:
.BR a \(en z
in this document,
.BR A \(en Z
to come back to from any.
.TP
.BI \(ga letter\fR,\fP\ \(aq letter
Go to a mark;
.B \(ga\(ga
goes back to where the last jump left from.
.TP
.BR Ctrl\-o ", " Ctrl\-i
Go back through the jumps, or forward again.
.SS Tabs
.TP 18
.BR gt ", " gT
Go to the next, previous tab; also
.BR Ctrl\-Tab ", " Ctrl\-Shift\-Tab .
With a count, go to tab
.IR n .
.TP
.B b
List the open tabs; type to narrow, Enter to go to one.
.TP
.B U
Open the tab closed last again.
.SS Copying
.TP 18
.B yy
Copy the path of the document's file.
.TP
.B yt
Copy the document's title, or its file name without one.
.TP
.B yp
Copy the text of the page in view, as running text, as
.B y
copies a selection.
.TP
.B yh
Copy the text of the marking picked.
.TP
.B yi
Label the pictures and figures in view: photographs, and drawings such as
charts and tables. Type a label to copy one to the clipboard as a picture.
.SH MOUSE
pdvim is read from the keyboard, but the mouse works too.
.TP
.B Wheel
Scroll; with
.BR Ctrl ,
zoom.
The
.B wheel\-speed
setting of pdvim(5) makes it faster or slower.
.TP
.B Click
Follow a link, or fill in a form field, as
.B f
does.
A click elsewhere ends the selection.
.TP
.B Drag
Over text, select it, as in visual mode:
.B y
copies it, and the keys of visual mode go on from there.
Begun off the pages, move them.
.TP
.B Double click
Select a word.
.TP
.B Middle drag
Move the pages.
.TP
.B Click in a list
Go to the tab, document, chapter, marking or page clicked, in the tab bar,
the lists, the table of contents, the markings, and the overview.
A click on the pages closes a list or panel shown over them.
.SH COMMANDS
Commands are typed after
.BR : .
Letters in brackets may be left out.
.B Tab
completes commands, settings, their values and file paths, and
.BR Up " and " Down
recall earlier command lines.
.B ;
separates commands run one after another.
Any action named in pdvim(5) runs as a command too, a number after it
standing for a count:
.BR ":zoom\-to 150" .
.TP
.BI : n
Go to page
.IR n .
.TP
.BI ":goto " label
Go to the page with that number printed on it, as
.B xii
or
.BR A\-3 .
.TP
.BI ":o[pen] " file
Open a document in a new tab.
.TP
.BI ":tabe[dit] " file "\fR, \fP:tabnew \fR[\fP" file \fR]\fP
Open a document in a new tab, or open an empty tab.
.TP
.BR ":tabn[ext] " [\fIn\fP] ", " :tabp[revious]
Go to the next tab, or tab
.IR n ,
or the previous.
.TP
.BR :tabc[lose] ", " :tabo[nly][!]
Close the tab, or every other tab.
.TP
.BR ":tabm[ove] " [\fIn\fP|+\fIn\fP|\-\fIn\fP]
Move the tab.
.TP
.BR :rec[ent]
List the documents read recently.
.TP
.BR ":h[elp] " [\fItopic\fP]
Show every action and the keys bound to it, at a mode, heading or action.
.TP
.BI ":apr[opos] " word
Show only the actions and keys that mention the word.
.TP
.BR ":man " [5]
Open this manual in a tab, or with
.BR 5 ,
pdvim(5), the manual of the configuration.
It is written out as a book in pdvim's data directory to be opened.
.TP
.BR :marks ", " ":delm[arks] " \fIletters\fP|!
List the marks, or take them away.
.TP
.BR :info ", " :f[ile]
Show the document's title, author, dates, pages, format and encryption.
.TP
.BI ":note " text
Put a note with that text in the margin at the top of the window.
.TP
.B :noh[lsearch]
Hide search highlights.
.TP
.BR :export[!] " \fIfile\fP"
Save the page in view as a
.B .png
image the size it is shown at, or an
.B .svg
drawing, turned as shown, in its own colours;
.B !
writes over a file there already.
.TP
.BI :! command
Run the rest of the line in the shell,
.B ;
and all.
Each
.B %
stands for the file of the document in view, quoted;
.B \e%
is a
.B %
itself.
pdvim goes on while it runs, and shows the last line it printed:
.B ":!xdg\-open %"
opens the document in another program.
.TP
.BI ":set " "setting value"
Change a setting, as listed in pdvim(5).
A boolean setting named without a value is turned on.
.TP
.BR ":theme " \fIname\fP|auto ", " :colo[rscheme]
Set the theme, or follow the system's; without a name, show it.
.TP
.BI ":map " "\fR[\fPmode\fR]\fP keys action\fR,\fP\ " ":unmap " "\fR[\fPmode\fR]\fP keys\fR,\fP\ " ":mapclear " \fR[\fPmode\fR]\fP
Bind keys to an action or to
.BI : command\fR,
remove a binding, or remove every binding of a mode.
.TP
.BR ":so[urce] " [\fIfile\fP]
Read the configuration file again, or another like it.
.TP
.BR :w[rite] ", " ":w[!] " \fIfile\fP
Save the markings into the file, or a copy with them into another.
.TP
.BR :wa[ll]
Save every document with markings not saved.
.TP
.BR :q[uit] ", " :qa[ll] ", " :q! ", " :qa!
Close the tab, or quit;
.B !
throws away markings not saved.
.TP
.BR :wq ", " :x ", " :wqa[ll] ", " :xa[ll]
Save, then close the tab, or quit.
.TP
.B :ve[rsion]
Show pdvim's version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pdvim/config.toml
The configuration, described in pdvim(5); by default
.IR ~/.config/pdvim/config.toml .
pdvim works without it.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/pdvim/history.sqlite
Where each document was left, its marks, the commands and searches typed,
and the tabs open at the last quit; by default
.IR ~/.local/share/pdvim/history.sqlite .
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/pdvim/manual/
The manual pages as HTML, written there by
.B :man
to be opened as books.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where to look for
.IR pdvim/config.toml .
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Where to keep
.IR pdvim/history.sqlite .
.TP
.B HOME
What
.B \(ti
stands for in paths, and where the two above are when not set.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when pdvim quits, and when it prints its version;
not 0 when the window could not be made.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pdvim (5),
.BR vi (1),
.BR zathura (1)
.PP
https://github.com/djmbritt/pdvim
